# Chroma content pack Stage and Screen: CROSSINGS and PARTS (the lead on every road), SIDE DOORS, EXITS, RIVALRY and
# REACH (moments 45 to 65 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md, Part 4). Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules:
# library-spec.md and the brief's pack rules. Names in title:, grants:, drops:, takes:, requires:, holds: and aims: come
# from stage/catalogue.py, core/reach.py and the base catalogue. Each life event is even on its own: alpha even, stakes
# 1.0, the five one-color options plus the combination set named in its header comment. per_year is the yearly rate of
# acting on the chance per eligible person (whoever holds the title or perk in holds:), estimates unless a source is
# named. Parts carry their real chances (the brief's pack rules), the same for every color inside a moment; the
# earned-odds lifts (helps.py, half on the lead) come on top in the engine. A lead for someone who may not hold an
# actor's title is `title: lead actor or actress; professional actor`, the lead first; the amateur, child and fringe lead
# is `grants: a lead role to remember`. Every moment carries earth:, tribal: and magic: lines in the forms of
# stage/worlds.py, and an untagged scene for each of the two other worlds; an option whose act reads differently there
# has its own wording. Children act only in supervised, licensed forms: a parent or carer who agrees, a chaperone,
# school hours kept. No real person, production, theatre, studio, film, award, agency or union is named.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (checklist P14, value tags): 17 options in this file retagged so the v: values read one of the
# option's own colors (check.py section 4): White's by-the-book singles lead with conformity (not conformity with
# achievement, which reads Blue), Red's singles on impulse lead with stimulation or hedonism (not self-direction,
# which reads Black), Green's singles of home and kin with tradition and benevolence or universalism (not security,
# which reads White), and Red's kindness on the spot with benevolence and stimulation; plus the largest
# disagreements check.py listed (an old custom asked: tradition and conformity; family first: benevolence and
# tradition; kin led away from the chief: self-direction and tradition; a day's pay weighed: power).

# ================================================================ crossings and parts

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 45. the showcase for agents (pairs D1a)
# Every color has its own way to a first job at 30 (a showcase graduate's real chance is 40 to 50): four with an agent
# who signs on the night, Green's by going home to the regional theatre. The pairs are the other honest roads out of
# drama school: an agency from home, the fringe, the year group, a mentor, and a word about who showcases favour.

== the showcase for agents
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult
age: 18-40
holds: drama school student | drama school diploma
tenure: 1-100
per_year: 0 0 .3 .1 0 0
drivers: prosper+.3
times: per final-year drama school student: once, in the last year; about 700 to 1,000 acting graduates a year leave accredited UK schools, and most sign with an agent at or soon after the showcase (estimate)
likelier: the final year of the course, a good year for theatre and television, a showcase scene that suits the student
rarer: a student who left the course early, a lean year with few agents in the seats, a graduate long out of school
gap: 2-4
domain: career.5
rite: young_adult
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: week
roles: mentor, friend, colleague, parent, rival
earth: the graduates' showcase in a studio theatre, an agent in every seat
tribal: the apprentices play before the elders of every band at the gathering
magic: the guild school's final masque, with the brokers and the company masters in the gallery
scene: Thirty graduates, two minutes each, and a hundred agents and casting directors in the dark with their notebooks. {friend} is in the scene before {Ns}, and {mentor} is pacing at the back of the room.
scene W: {N} has rehearsed the two minutes until every move is fixed. The scene belongs to the partner in it as much as to {N}, and {N} means to play it that way.
scene U: {N} chose the speech for what it shows: range, stillness, and a turn in the middle that the room will not see coming. Every second has been weighed.
scene B: {N} has the list of who is coming and knows which three agents matter. The rest of the room is scenery.
scene R: {N} cannot feel {Ns} hands. The lights go down, the room goes quiet, and something inside wants to burst out into the dark.
scene G: {Ns} family came down on the early train and sits at the back among the agents, in their best coats, holding a programme with {Ns} name in it.
scene tribal: At the gathering the old tellers' apprentices play in turn by the great fire, and the elders of every band watch to see whom they will ask to winter with them.
scene magic: The guild school's final masque is played in the long hall, and the brokers and company masters lean over the gallery rail with their wax tablets.
- W | play the scene exactly as rehearsed, for the partner and not for the room | title: professional actor | grants: an agent who believes in you | v: conformity | tribal: play the telling exactly as the old teller taught it, for the other player and not for the elders | magic: play the scene exactly as rehearsed, for the partner and not for the gallery | chance: 30
- U | choose the speech that shows a range no other graduate can show | title: professional actor | grants: an agent who believes in you | aims: professional actor | v: achievement | tribal: choose the telling that shows every voice the old teller gave you | magic: choose the speech that shows a range no other scholar can show | chance: 30
- B | stay at the drinks until the three agents who matter know the name | title: professional actor | grants: an agent who believes in you | v: power, achievement | tribal: stay by the elders' fire until the three who matter know your name | magic: stay in the gallery until the three brokers who matter know your name | chance: 30
- R | drop the safe speech at the last minute, and play the one that burns | title: professional actor | grants: an agent who believes in you | mark: took a wild risk | v: stimulation | tribal: drop the safe telling at the last moment, and play the one that burns | chance: 30
- G | skip the agents' drinks, and ask the theatre back home for a season | title: professional actor | aims: professional actor | mark: came home | v: tradition, security | tribal: go back to your own band, and ask to be its teller-player for the winter | magic: go back to your home city, and ask its playhouse for a season | chance: 30
- BG | sign with the small agency from home that asks first, and look no further | grants: an agent who believes in you | binds | v: security, power | tribal: take the go-between from your own band who asks first, and look no further | magic: sign with the first broker who asks, an old hand from your home city, and look no further | chance: 70
- BR | skip the agents, and book a fringe slot with two classmates instead | grants: a fringe slot | requires: savings | without: means | lacking: .5 | door | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: skip the elders' fire, and take a place by the outer fires with two others | magic: skip the gallery, and hire a booth at the summer fair with two classmates | chance: 72
- UG | promise the year group to keep making work together, whatever happens | grants: a year group from drama school | binds | v: benevolence, universalism | tribal: promise the others who learned beside you to keep telling together | magic: promise your year at the guild school to keep playing together | chance: 70
- WR | say out loud to the school that the showcase favours students with money | mark: defied an authority | identity | v: universalism, self-direction | tribal: say out loud at the fire that the elders only ask for the sons and daughters of great hearths | magic: say out loud that the masque favours scholars with rich patrons | chance: 72
- WU | ask the voice teacher to stay on as a mentor after the course ends | grants: mentor | door | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: ask the old teller to keep guiding you after the three winters end | magic: ask the master of voice to keep teaching you after the seal is given | chance: 72
works: An agent from the third row finds {N} at the drinks, and by the end of the month there is a contract and a first audition.
works: The two minutes go as well as they ever went in rehearsal, and the next morning {Ns} phone holds three messages.
fails: Half the agents leave before the drinks, and {Ns} name is on nobody's list.
fails: {N} dries for a moment in the middle of the speech, and the room's attention drifts on to the next scene.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 46. cast as understudy to the lead (pairs D1b)
# The cover is won at the real chance for a professional actor who is put up for it (25 to 35), one way in every color;
# the pairs are the honest ways of weighing it, turning it down or getting ready for it.

== cast as understudy to the lead
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 17-90
holds: professional actor
per_year: 0 0 .08 .06 .03 .01
drivers: prosper+.2
times: per professional actor a year: about 1 in 15 young actors are offered a cover; long runs cover every lead, and about 1 professional actor in 3 understudies at some point (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: a long run with a big cast, good years for the theatres, an actor who learns lines fast, a director who has worked with the actor before
rarer: a short run, a small company that cannot pay for covers, an actor known only for screen work, old age
gap: 2-4
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, friend, partner, rival
earth: a long-running musical needs a cover for the lead
tribal: the elders want someone who can wear the great mask if its wearer falls
magic: the court playhouse needs a second for its leading player for the winter season
scene: The casting office rings: the long-running musical needs a cover for the lead, with a place in the ensemble, for a year. Eight shows a week at the back of the stage, and some nights, perhaps, the front of it.
scene W: A cover is there so the show never stops. {N} thinks of the people who will have paid for the lead and got the cover instead, and means to give them a night they will not regret.
scene U: {N} has seen the lead three times and has already noticed where the part could go deeper, if anyone ever let it.
scene B: The biggest show in town, from the inside. {N} wonders who in the building decides what comes next, and how to be in that room.
scene R: A year at the back of the chorus, waiting for someone else to fall ill? Part of {N} wants to laugh, and part of {N} is already singing the big number in the kitchen.
scene G: {N} thinks of a whole year with one company: the same dressing room, the same walk to the stage door, the same faces at the half. It could feel like home.
scene tribal: The elders want someone who can wear the great mask if its wearer falls sick before the summer gathering. They look round the fire, and their eyes stop on {N}.
scene magic: The court playhouse needs a second for its leading player for the winter season: someone who knows every line and waits in the wings every night, in case.
- W | say yes, and be word-perfect in the part before the first rehearsal | title: understudy | grants: learning lines | self_control: + | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: say yes, and know the great telling word for word before the next moon | chance: 30
- U | go to the recall with the lead's part worked out scene by scene | title: understudy | aims: lead actor or actress | v: achievement | tribal: come before the elders with the great telling learned by heart, every pause in its place | magic: come to the second trial with the leading part studied scene by scene | chance: 29
- B | take the cover, and get the producers to promise a look at the next lead | title: understudy | v: power, achievement | tribal: take the place, and get the elders to promise a look at the next great mask that falls free | magic: take the place, and get the play-merchant to promise a look at the next leading part | chance: 28
- R | sing the lead's big number at the audition as if it were opening night | title: understudy | body: light | v: stimulation, hedonism | tribal: dance the ancestor's telling before the elders as if the gathering had already begun | magic: sing the leading player's great song at the trial as if the court were watching | chance: 32
- G | take it, and settle into the company for the whole long run | title: understudy | v: security, tradition | tribal: take it, and settle in among the band's tellers for the whole year | magic: take it, and settle into the court company for the whole winter | chance: 31
- RG | turn it down for a part in a friend's new play above a pub | mark: turned down a chance | v: self-direction, benevolence | tribal: turn it down to play in a friend's new telling at the next fire | magic: turn it down for a part in a friend's new piece at a tavern playhouse | chance: 72
- UB | work out what a year of covering is worth against the screen work it blocks | v: achievement, power | tribal: work out what a year waiting for the mask is worth against the hunts it would cost | magic: work out what a season as second is worth against the glamour work it blocks | chance: 78
- UR | learn the whole part in a week, just to see if it can be done | grants: learning lines | self_control: + | habit | v: stimulation, achievement | tribal: learn the whole great telling in seven nights, just to see if it can be done | chance: 72
- WB | turn it down, to honour the contract already signed with a regional theatre | mark: kept your word | v: conformity, security | tribal: turn it down, to keep the winter you promised another band | magic: turn it down, to honour the season already signed with a city playhouse | chance: 74
- WG | turn it down, because a year away would be hard on the family | mark: stayed home | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: turn it down, because a year at the great fire would take you from your own hearth | chance: 74
works: The casting office rings back within the week, and by Monday {N} is learning a part {N} may never get to play, and loving it.
works: Whatever {N} decides, it is decided well, and the next year has a shape {N} can live with.
fails: The cover goes to someone who sings higher, and the casting office is kind about it.
fails: The choice sits badly for months, and {N} keeps wondering about the other road.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 47. the understudy goes on (pairs D2a)
# The cover goes on; being kept on as the lead comes at the real chance (15 to 25), the same in every color. The pairs
# are what else the night can bring: a voice saved, a risk taken, a drink (never forced), the company held together,
# a name for being ready.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (checklist P5): per_year .5 to .4 (the lead ran at 3x its target).

== the understudy goes on
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 17-95
holds: understudy
per_year: 0 0 .4 .4 .4 .25
drivers: fortune+.4
times: per understudy of a year or more as a professional actor: about 2 in 5 go on at least once in a run; about 1 in 5 who go on are kept on or given a lead soon after (estimate)
likelier: a long run, a lead who is ill or injured, a heavy show eight times a week, winter colds in the company
rarer: a short run, a lead who never misses a show, a second cover ahead in the queue
gap: 1-2
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, friend, partner, mentor
earth: an announcement before the curtain: at this performance the part will be played by...
tribal: the wearer of the great mask lies sick, and the mask is handed to you at dusk
magic: the leading player's carriage has overturned, and the herald cries your name instead
scene: At four o'clock the company manager rings: the lead has lost a voice, and {N} is on tonight. By the half the costume has been taken in, the announcement is written, and {partner} is somewhere up in the circle.
scene W: {N} has done this part in an empty theatre every Thursday for months. Tonight it is for the people who paid, and {N} owes them the show they came for.
scene U: {N} has watched every performance from the wings and knows where the lead rushes, where a laugh is lost, and what could be done with the second act.
scene B: The producers will hear about tonight by morning. {N} has already made sure the agent knows, and the casting director too.
scene R: {Ns} heart is going like a drum. This is it: the part {N} has sung in the shower for a year, in front of a full house.
scene G: The company gathers in the wings before curtain up: the dressers, the stage manager, the old hands from the chorus. They all want {N} to fly.
scene tribal: The wearer of the great mask lies sick in his shelter, and at dusk the elders bring the mask to {N}. The whole gathering waits by the fire.
scene magic: The leading player's carriage has overturned on the bridge, and the herald steps out before the curtain to cry {Ns} name instead. The court murmurs in the boxes.
- W | play it exactly as rehearsed, every move and every note | title: lead actor or actress | grants: good notices | v: conformity | tribal: play the telling exactly as the elders set it, every step and every cry | chance: 20
- U | play it with the changes worked out over a year in the wings | title: lead actor or actress | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: play it with the changes worked out over a year of watching from behind the screens | magic: play it with the changes worked out over a season in the wings | chance: 20
- B | make sure the producers and the agent are in the house tonight | title: lead actor or actress | aims: lead actor or actress | v: power, achievement | tribal: make sure the elders of every band are at the fire tonight | magic: make sure the patron and the broker are in the boxes tonight | chance: 20
- R | forget the blocking, and play it the way it has burned in you all year | title: lead actor or actress | mark: took a wild risk | v: stimulation | tribal: forget the steps the elders set, and play it the way it has burned in you all year | chance: 21
- G | go on calmly, as if it were one more night in the long run | title: lead actor or actress | v: security, tradition | tribal: go out to the fire calmly, as if it were one more night of the winter tellings | magic: go on calmly, as if it were one more night of the season | chance: 20
- BG | rest the voice all afternoon, and save it for the big number | self_control: + | v: security, achievement | tribal: rest the voice all day, and save it for the ancestor's great cry | magic: rest the voice all afternoon, and save it for the great song | chance: 78
- BR | throw in new business the director never approved, and see if it lands | grants: improvisation | v: stimulation, power | tribal: throw in a new turn the elders never saw, and see if the gathering roars | magic: throw in a new piece of business the master never approved, and see if the court laughs | chance: 72
- UR | have a large drink in the dressing room to steady the nerves | self_control: - | closed: approval: drinking before a show; backfire: the company manager smells it, and the producers never use the cover again | v: hedonism | tribal: drink the strong brew in the shelter to steady the nerves | magic: drink a large glass of wine in the tiring-room to steady the nerves | chance: 75
- WG | hold the show together for the company, exactly as the director asked | grants: a director who keeps casting you | mark: kept your word | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: hold the telling together for the other players, exactly as the shaper asked | magic: hold the play together for the company, exactly as the master of the play asked | chance: 72
- WU | go in early, and run every scene with the stage manager before the half | grants: reliable record | self_control: + | habit | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: come to the fire early, and walk every step of the telling with the keeper of the fire | magic: come in early, and run every scene with the book-keeper before the bell | chance: 76
works: The producers come round after the curtain, and by Friday {N} has the part for the rest of the run.
works: The house stands at the end, and the company lines the corridor to cheer {N} back to the dressing room.
fails: The lead is back on Monday, and {N} goes back to the wings with one good night to remember.
fails: A missed cue in the second act throws a scene, and the notes the next day are polite and short.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 48. the part of a lifetime is cast (pairs D2b)
# A great part or a film that wants an unknown: the lead comes at the real chance for someone who is seen (4 to 8), one
# way in every color, for a working actor, or an amateur of five years with a lead behind them (the non-professional
# cast in a lead, real but rare: engine-conditions.py LIFE_STAGE). Without a listing in the casting directory a
# person is seen only at an open call, so those acts keep three tenths of their chance (lacking .3).

== the part of a lifetime is cast
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-100
holds: professional actor | voice actor | understudy | amateur actor
per_year: 0 0 .02 .015 .01 .006
drivers: era+.2 fortune+.3
times: per professional or voice actor of two years or more, or amateur of five years or more with a lead behind them, a year: about 1 in 50 young adults and fewer later try for a part that could change their life; about 1 in 15 of those who try get it (estimate)
likelier: a film that wants an unknown face, a famous old play revived with a new lead, a part written for someone of the person's age and kind, a good agent
rarer: a part already promised to a star, a life far from the cities where films are cast, no listing in the casting directory
gap: 3-8
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work, meaning
horizon: months
roles: friend, mentor, partner, rival, boss
earth: the casting call for a famous old play's great part, or a film that wants a face nobody knows
tribal: the clans will tell the oldest story at the gathering, and every band sends a player for the first ancestor
magic: the court will stage the old epic of the realm, and the master of the play is looking everywhere
scene: The casting call is everywhere at once: a film of a famous old story wants a face nobody knows for its great part, any age, any background, actors and non-actors alike. {friend} sends the link to {N} with three exclamation marks.
scene W: {N} has loved this part since school and knows what it asks: years of discipline in one performance. If it is to be done, it must be done properly.
scene U: {N} reads the call twice and then the old play itself, all night, looking for the person inside the part.
scene B: Parts like this make careers, and they are cast through people. {N} starts working out who knows the casting director.
scene R: {N} feels it the moment the call is read: this part, this person, now. Nothing has felt so clear in years.
scene G: The part is an old farmer from a small place who never left the valley. {N} has known people like that all {Ns} life.
scene tribal: The clans will tell the oldest story at the gathering, and every band must send a player for the first ancestor. At {Ns} fire the families look at one another, and then at {N}.
scene magic: The court will stage the old epic of the realm, and the master of the play is said to be looking in every tavern and every guild hall for a face the realm has never seen.
- W | prepare the audition for months, and arrive word-perfect on the day | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | requires: casting directory listing | without: approval | lacking: .3 | self_control: + | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: prepare the ancestor's telling for moons, and come to the elders word-perfect | magic: prepare the trial piece for months, and come before the master word-perfect | chance: 6
- U | read everything about the play and its first actors, and build the part from it | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | requires: casting directory listing | without: approval | lacking: .3 | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: learn every way the oldest story has ever been told, and build the ancestor from them | magic: read every chronicle of the old epic and its first players, and build the part from them | chance: 6
- B | get the agent to put the name in front of the casting director first | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | requires: casting directory listing | without: approval | lacking: .3 | aims: lead actor or actress | v: power | tribal: get the go-between to speak your name to the keeper of the tellings first | magic: get the broker to put your name before the master of the play first | chance: 6
- R | walk into the audition and play it straight from the heart, without a note | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | requires: casting directory listing | without: approval | lacking: .3 | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: walk up to the elders and play the ancestor straight from the heart | magic: walk into the trial and play it straight from the heart, without a note | chance: 6
- G | play it plain and true, as the old people of home would know it | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | requires: casting directory listing | without: approval | lacking: .3 | identity | v: tradition | tribal: play it plain and true, as the old ones of your band would know it | magic: play it plain and true, as the old folk of your village would know it | chance: 6
- RG | claim on the form that you can ride, then learn on a cousin's farm | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a lie on the casting form; backfire: the director asks to see the riding on the first day, and the part goes elsewhere | v: achievement, stimulation | tribal: tell the elders you can dance the bear dance, and learn it in half a moon from a cousin | magic: swear to the master you can ride, and learn in a fortnight on a cousin's farm | chance: 80
- UB | send a polished self-tape, and spend the waiting time on other auditions | v: achievement, security | tribal: show the elders your telling once, and spend the waiting on the winter hunts | magic: send a glamour-sketch of the scene, and spend the waiting on other trials | chance: 82
- UG | go along for the day, and learn what you can from watching the others | grants: auditioning | door | v: self-direction, achievement | tribal: go to the gathering, and learn what you can from watching the other players | magic: go to the trial, and learn what you can from watching the other players | chance: 80
- WB | withdraw, because the dates clash with a job already signed for | mark: kept your word | v: conformity, security | tribal: step back, because the winter is promised to another band | magic: withdraw, because the dates clash with a season already signed | chance: 82
- WR | help a friend prepare for it, and drive them to the open call | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence | tribal: help a friend prepare the ancestor's telling, and walk with them to the gathering | magic: help a friend prepare the trial piece, and walk with them to the court | chance: 80
works: The casting director rings on a Thursday night, and {Ns} life divides into before and after.
works: Whatever comes of the call, {N} meets it the way {N} chose, and is glad of it a year later.
fails: The part goes to someone else, as it nearly always does, and {N} reads the news like everyone else.
fails: {N} gets as far as the last six, and the call that comes is kind and short.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 49. a screen test (pairs D3a)
# A test for the lead of a new series turns into the part about 1 time in 10 (8 to 12) for a working actor, one way in
# every color; the test comes only to a working actor (professional or voice), so the first paid part comes first.
# A lead in a series makes the face known (the catalogue: [a known face] from 'a screen test' won). The pairs are the
# supporting part, notes, practice, a road back to the stage, and a dubbed self-tape.

== a screen test
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-95
holds: professional actor | voice actor
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 .04 .03 .02 .01
drivers: era+.3 prosper+.2
times: per professional or voice actor of two years or more a year: about 1 in 25 young adults are tested for a screen part; about 1 test in 10 becomes the lead (estimate)
likelier: a boom in new series, an agent with screen contacts, a showreel, a face that suits the part, years of work in front of a camera
rarer: a lean year for television, a life far from where series are made, no showreel, an actor known only for stage work
gap: 2-5
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, mentor, colleague, partner, rival
earth: a screen test for the lead in a new series
tribal: the shaper of the shadow telling wants to see your shape against the wall
magic: the illusionist wants to see if the glamour takes to your face
scene: A studio on an industrial estate, a camera on a dolly, a director with a coffee, and two scenes and a song to play with a reader who has done this forty times today. The lead in a new series about a band on the road is still not cast.
scene W: {N} has learned both scenes until they are part of the furniture, and asks the camera operator for the marks before anything else.
scene U: {N} knows that on screen less is more. The camera sees a thought before the face moves, and the whole test is in the eyes.
scene B: The producers will watch the tape tonight with three others. {N} is thinking about how to be the one they remember.
scene R: The camera is so close that {N} can see {Ns} own face in the lens. {N} wants to forget the lens entirely and just be the person in the scene.
scene G: {N} found the character in someone from home: the way an uncle stood at the bar, the way an old neighbour said goodbye.
scene tribal: The shaper of the shadow telling has the fire built high behind the hide screen. {N} must play the hunter in silhouette, and every small move is thrown huge across the cave wall.
scene magic: The illusionist lays a glamour across {Ns} face to see if it takes: the audience must see the hero in the air above the stage, as large as a house.
- W | learn both scenes perfectly, and hit every mark the crew gives | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | grants: a known face | v: conformity | tribal: learn both scenes perfectly, and stand exactly where the shaper's fire throws the shadow | magic: learn both scenes perfectly, and stand exactly where the glamour is set | chance: 10
- U | play it small for the lens, and let the camera find the thought | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | grants: a known face | v: achievement | tribal: play it small for the wall, and let the fire find every gesture | magic: play it small for the glamour, and let the spell find the thought | chance: 10
- B | get the agent to bring the producers to the test, and play it to them | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | grants: a known face | aims: lead actor or actress | v: power, achievement | tribal: get the go-between to bring the elders of the gathering, and play it to them | magic: get the broker to bring the patron to the trial, and play it to the patron | chance: 10
- R | ignore the notes, and play the scene the way it feels in the moment | title: lead actor or actress; professional actor | grants: a known face | mark: took a wild risk | v: stimulation, hedonism | tribal: ignore the shaper's signs, and play the hunt the way it feels in the moment | chance: 10
