scene: The regional company's leading actor has taken a part in a television series and leaves at the end of the month, half way through the season. Three plays still need their lead, and {boss} calls the company together after the matinee.
scene W: {N} has played beside the leading part all season and knows it as well as {Ns} own. The company needs the gap filled, and {N} could fill it.
scene U: {N} has thought for months about how the three parts could be played, and now the question is no longer only in {Ns} head.
scene B: A leading part, a leading player's money, and the season's posters. {N} knows exactly what this vacancy is worth, and to whom.
scene R: {N} wants it so badly that the matinee notes go past unheard. The bar after the show will decide everything.
scene G: {N} came up through this company: the school tours, the holiday shows, the walk-on parts. This is the place {N} belongs, if it will have {N} at the front.
scene tribal: The teller who always wore the great mask has gone to live with his wife's people beyond the river. The midsummer telling has no ancestor, and the shaper looks round the fire.
scene magic: The company's leading player has been lured to the court playhouse by a noble patron. The wagons leave for the next city in a week, and the master of the play needs a new lead before the road.
