scene: The touring company's stage manager has left in the middle of the run, and the company manager rings {N} on a Thursday: the show is in a new town on Monday, the prompt book is a mess, and someone has to call the cues.
scene W: Somebody has to keep the show running, and {N} knows the cues better than anyone outside the booth. It feels like a duty.
scene U: {N} has always wondered how the deputy manages forty cues in the storm scene. Now there is a chance to find out from the inside.
scene B: A stage manager is the person in the building who knows everything. {N} can see what that would be worth, and what it should pay.
scene R: A new town every week, a van full of scenery and a headset in the dark. Part of {N} wants to go tonight.
scene G: The tour is away for eleven weeks, and {N} thinks of the people at home, and of the local players' play in May, which needs {N} too.
