scene: The regional theatre's artistic director is stepping down after ten years, and the board has advertised the post: the building, the money, the seasons, the choice of who plays what for years to come. {Ns} name has been mentioned in the bar more than once.
scene W: The theatre has served the town well for ten years. Whoever takes it on owes the town the same care, and {N} knows the board, the staff and the audience by name.
scene U: {N} has seen every show in the building for a decade and knows what is missing from it: the new plays, the plays that would bring in the people who never come.
scene B: The post decides who works in the region for ten years. {N} counts the board members who would back {N}, and the ones who would need persuading.
scene R: A whole theatre to fill with whatever {N} wants to see. The thought is like standing at the top of a high diving board.
scene G: It is the theatre {N} was taken to as a child, the one with the velvet seats and the smell of oranges. {N} would want it to stay the town's own.
scene tribal: The keeper of the gathering's tellings has grown too old to walk to the great fire, and the elders of every band will choose another. {Ns} name is spoken at more than one fire.
scene magic: The court playhouse's master has died in his chair, and the Order will choose another to run the house and its seasons. {Ns} name is said in the players' room more than once.
