roles: boss, colleague, friend, partner
earth: the school's drama teacher has left, and the head asks a teacher who acts to take the subject on
tribal: the old teller who taught the children has grown too frail to teach, and the elders ask someone who knows the tellings to take it on
magic: the town school has lost its master of the players' art, and the schoolmaster is asked to take the class
scene: The drama teacher has left at the end of the autumn term for a job in another city, and the school play is in March. The head stops {N} in the corridor: everyone knows about the local players' shows, so would {N} take drama on, for the spring at least, and perhaps for good?
scene W: A subject needs a syllabus, a scheme of work and somebody who will be there every week. {N} could do it, if it is done properly.
scene U: {N} has always thought drama could be taught like any craft: voice, text and movement, step by step, until a shy child can stand in front of a room and be heard.
scene B: Head of drama means a department, a budget and a line on the timetable. {N} wonders what the head would give to have the school play saved.
scene R: The school play! {N} is already imagining a musical: the whole year group on stage, a band in the pit, the hall roaring.
scene G: There are children in every class who never get chosen for anything. {N} thinks of them first, and of the school hall on Saturday mornings, empty.
scene tribal: The old teller who taught the children the tellings has grown too frail to sit out in the cold, and the small ones sit at the fire with nobody to teach them the voices and the masks. The elders look at {N}, who has told at the turning seasons for years.
scene magic: The town school's master of the players' art has gone to a guild school in the capital, and the masque is at midsummer. The schoolmaster asks {N}, who plays with the ward's mystery players, to take the class.
- W | take it on properly, with a scheme of work and a course in the holidays | title: drama teacher | self_control: + | v: conformity, security | tribal: take on the children's tellings properly, one telling for each moon | magic: take on the players' class properly, with a plan for every week | chance: 30
- U | take it on as a craft, and teach voice, text and movement step by step | title: drama teacher | identity | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: teach the children the voices, the masks and the steps, one at a time | magic: teach the players' art as a craft, voice, text and movement, step by step | chance: 30
- B | take it on as head of drama, with a department and a budget of its own | title: drama teacher | binds | v: power, achievement | tribal: take on the teaching, and ask for a teller's share of every hunt for it | magic: take the class as its master, with a stipend and a room of its own | chance: 30
- R | direct the school musical this once, with the whole year group, and hand drama back | grants: directing actors | mark: took a wild risk | v: stimulation, benevolence | tribal: shape one great telling with every child of the band, and hand the teaching back after | magic: stage one great masque with the whole school, and hand the class back after | chance: 30
- G | start a Saturday youth theatre in the school hall, open to every child in town | title: community theatre director | habit | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: gather the children at the fire on the quiet nights, every child of the band welcome | magic: start a feast-day players' class in the school hall, open to every child in the ward | chance: 30
- WB | ask the head for a budget and a proper studio before giving an answer | grants: organising people | v: security, conformity | tribal: ask the elders for a sheltered place and hides for masks before giving an answer | magic: ask the schoolmaster for a stipend and a proper room before giving an answer | chance: 70
- WG | say no, and hand the class to a colleague who trained in drama | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence, universalism | tribal: say no, and send the elders to a teller of the next band who knows the children's tellings | magic: say no, and put forward a colleague who studied at the guild school | chance: 70
- UB | take an evening course in teaching drama first, and give an answer next year | grants: teaching | aims: drama teacher | v: achievement, security | tribal: sit with the teller of the next band for a winter first, and give an answer at the thaw | magic: take the guild school's evening course first, and give an answer next year | chance: 70
- UR | write a play for the year group, with a part for every child who wants one | grants: writing scripts | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: make a telling for the children, with a voice for every child who wants one | magic: write a masque for the class, with a part for every child who wants one | chance: 70
- RG | take the class to see the local players, and let the children decide | mark: made a friend | v: stimulation, tradition | tribal: take the children to the band's midwinter telling, and let them choose their spirits | magic: take the class to the ward's mystery play, and let the children decide | chance: 70
works: The school play goes on in March after all, every seat taken, and by the summer drama has a room of its own and a waiting list.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the children have a stage this year, and {N} is glad of the part played in it.
fails: The head gives drama to a newly qualified teacher from outside, and thanks {N} warmly in the staff room.
fails: The spring term swallows everything, the play is cut to a reading in the library, and {N} promises to do it properly next year.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 63. two actors, one part (triads D4a)
# The last two at the recall: [lead actor or actress] at 16 to 18 in every color, Black's way through the producer
# who backs the person (an enemy made on the way). The triads carry the rivalry's other roads, none forced: reporting
# the rival by the book, a whispered lie, stepping aside for the friend, running lines together, refusing a third
# recall. The rivalry options give no title.

== two actors, one part
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 17-95
holds: professional actor | understudy
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 .1 .08 .05 .03
drivers: fortune+.1 ties+.1
times: per professional actor of two years or more a year: about 1 in 10 reach the last two for a lead (estimate)
likelier: a busy year for new productions, an actor whose name is going round, an understudy who went on and was seen
rarer: a production cast with a famous face from the start, a lean year, an actor out of the city
gap: 1-3
domain: career.5
tone: mixed
life: work, friends
horizon: week
roles: rival, friend, boss, colleague, partner
earth: two actors left at the recall, and one part
tribal: two players want the great mask, and the elders will choose at dawn
magic: two players are left for the court lead, and the patron will choose
scene: The third recall, on a Friday afternoon: {N} and {rival} are the last two for the lead, and the director will choose on Monday. They were at drama school together, and they have been going for the same parts for ten years.
scene W: There is a right way to win a part: be ready, be on time, and let the work speak. {N} means to do it that way, whatever happens.
scene U: {N} has read the scene forty times and found something in it nobody else has seen: the line in the middle where the character lies, and knows it.
scene B: A producer who has backed {N} before is putting money into this show. One phone call would tip the scales, and everyone in the business makes them.
scene R: {N} wants this more than anything in years, and {rival} knows it, and {N} knows that {rival} wants it just as much.
scene G: {rival} was the first friend {N} made in the city, the one who lent the rent in the first hard winter. Now there is one part, and two of them.
scene tribal: Two players want the great mask for the summer, {N} and {rival}, and the elders will choose at dawn. They grew up at the same fire and learned the tellings from the same old woman.
scene magic: Two players are left for the court lead, {N} and {rival}, and the patron will choose after the last hearing. They came up through the same guild school, a year apart.
- W | play the scene exactly as the director asked, and let the work decide | title: lead actor or actress | identity | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: tell it exactly as the elders asked, and let the telling decide | magic: play it exactly as the patron's master asked, and let the work decide | chance: 16
- U | prepare the scene all week, every beat worked out | title: lead actor or actress | self_control: + | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: prepare the telling every night until dawn, every breath worked out | chance: 18
- B | ring the producer who has backed the work before, and ask for a word | title: lead actor or actress | requires: a producer who backs you | without: approval | lacking: .3 | mark: made an enemy | v: power, achievement | tribal: ask the elder who has always favoured the telling to speak at the choosing | magic: send a note to the patron's steward, who has always favoured the work | chance: 17
- R | play it as if nothing else in the world mattered | title: lead actor or actress | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 18
- G | play it the way the old company taught, honest and plain | title: lead actor or actress | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: tell it the way the old woman taught, honest and plain | magic: play it the way the guild school taught, honest and plain | chance: 17
- WUR | run lines with the rival the night before, as a kindness | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence, universalism | tribal: practise the telling with the rival the night before, as a kindness | chance: 76
- WUB | report the rival's missed rehearsal call to the company manager | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: reporting a rival; backfire: the company sees why it was done, and the director chooses the rival | v: conformity, power | tribal: tell the elders that the rival missed the gathering of the tellers | magic: report the rival's missed hearing to the patron's steward | chance: 72
- WBG | step aside, so the old friend can have the part | mark: made a friend | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: step aside, so the old friend can wear the mask | chance: 78
- URG | whisper to the director's assistant that the rival drinks | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a whispered lie about a rival; backfire: the company finds out who started it, and nobody will share a dressing room with {N} | v: power, achievement | tribal: whisper to the elders' runner that the rival sleeps through the dawn tellings | magic: whisper to the patron's steward that the rival drinks | chance: 72
- BRG | refuse a third recall, and walk away with pride intact | mark: turned down a chance | door | v: self-direction, power | tribal: refuse to tell again at dawn, and walk away with pride intact | chance: 72
works: The director rings on Monday morning, and {N} sits on the kitchen floor for a while before calling anyone.
works: Whatever {N} chose, {N} can look {rival} in the eye at the first night, and does.
fails: The part goes to {rival}, and the director says it came down to a coin's weight.
fails: The production is postponed a year, and by the time it is cast again both of them have moved on.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 64. the show is a hit (triads D4b)
# Reach for the lead: [an award for acting] for a lead on White's single at 10 (impossible without the lead title),
# and [known across the country] at 11 or 12 on the other singles. The triads are a producer who backs the person, a
# season more with the company as promised, small work back home, taking the credit for the company's ideas (a wrong),
# and the move to the biggest theatre at once.

== the show is a hit
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-100
holds: lead actor or actress | playwright or screenwriter | director | producer
per_year: 0 0 .03 .03 .03 .02
drivers: prosper+.2 fortune+.15 era+.1
times: per lead, writer, director or producer a year: about 1 in 30 have a show that sells out and moves on; only a few of those become known across the country (estimate)
likelier: good years for the theatres, a show that catches the mood of the year, a reviewer who loves it, a run long enough for word to spread
rarer: a lean year, a short run in a small house, a show that opens in the same week as a famous one
gap: 3-8
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work, publiclife, money
horizon: months
roles: colleague, partner, friend, boss, rival
earth: queues round the block, a sold-out run and a transfer
tribal: every band in the valley asks for the telling
magic: the court asks for a command performance
scene: The show has sold out for its whole run, there is a queue for returns round the block every evening, and a producer wants to move it to the biggest theatre in the capital. {Ns} name is in the papers on Sunday, spelled right for once.
scene W: {N} thinks of the company: the stage manager who held it together, the designer, the actor who never missed a cue. Whatever comes now, it was all of them.
scene U: {N} knows exactly why it works: the second act, the silence before the last scene. The trick now is to do it again, better.
scene B: For a few months, the doors are open that were shut for years. {N} knows they will not stay open long, and means to walk through as many as possible.
scene R: The applause at the curtain call goes on so long the cast runs out of bows. {N} has never felt anything like it.
scene G: The whole family came on the first Saturday, and {Ns} old drama teacher wrote a card. {N} keeps thinking of the small theatre where it all began.
scene tribal: Every band in the valley has sent a runner to ask for {Ns} telling at its own fire before the summer ends. Children at the far gathering act it out with sticks.
scene magic: The court has asked for a command performance of {Ns} play before the crowned heads. The broadsheets cry {Ns} name in the square.
- W | go to the national theatre awards, and thank the whole company on the night | grants: an award for acting | requires: lead actor or actress | without: impossible | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: stand before the elders of every band at the great fire, and name every teller who helped | magic: go before the court's laurel judges, and thank the whole company on the night | chance: 10
- U | give interviews about the craft, and never about the fame | grants: known across the country | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: speak at every fire about how the telling was made, never about the praise | magic: speak to the broadsheets about the craft, never about the fame | chance: 12
- B | say yes to every chat show and advert while the name is hot | grants: known across the country | self_control: - | v: power, achievement | tribal: go to every band that asks, and take every gift while the name is warm | magic: say yes to every salon and every patron while the name is hot | chance: 12
- R | party every night of the run with the famous people who now ring | grants: known across the country | self_control: - | v: hedonism, stimulation | tribal: feast every night of the summer with the chiefs who now send for the teller | magic: revel every night of the season with the great families who now send for the player | chance: 11
- G | take the show on tour to the towns that never get one | grants: known across the country | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: carry the telling to the far bands that never hear one | magic: take the play on the road to the towns that never see one | chance: 11
- WUG | go back to small work in the theatre where it all began | mark: came home | v: tradition, achievement | tribal: go back to the small fire where the telling began | magic: go back to the little playhouse where it all began | chance: 72
- WRG | stay with the company for the next season, as promised | mark: kept your word | binds | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: stay with the band's tellers for the next summer, as promised | chance: 74
- WBR | move the show to the biggest theatre in the capital at once | door | v: power, stimulation | tribal: take the telling to the great fire of all the bands at once | magic: move the play to the grandest playhouse in the capital at once | chance: 70
- UBR | take the credit in interviews for the company's best ideas | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: taking credit for others' work; backfire: the designer tells the papers whose ideas they were | v: power, achievement | tribal: take the praise at every fire for the other tellers' best moments | magic: take the credit with the broadsheets for the company's best ideas | chance: 70
- UBG | sign with a producer for the next three shows | grants: a producer who backs you | v: security, achievement | tribal: give the next three summers' tellings to a chief who will feast every teller | magic: sign with a patron for the next three plays | chance: 70
works: By the end of the run {Ns} face is on the side of buses, and strangers smile at {N} in the street as if they were old friends.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the success lasts long enough to change things, and {N} is still glad of how it was used.
fails: The transfer falls through over money, and the show closes when its run ends, a hit that went nowhere.
fails: The next production is announced, and nobody remembers to put {Ns} name on it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 65. the reviews are in (read event: five readings)
# What the lead reads in the morning, on every road. Tone mixed and one small gain on every reading, so the readings
# net out even: the hard readings (a game, a wound) carry a lower impact and a small gain of their own (autonomy, the
# company's warmth). The engine's condition is READ_STAGE in engine-conditions.py.

== the reviews are in
tier: read
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
holds: professional actor | lead actor or actress | director | playwright or screenwriter | amateur actor | community theatre director
source: career
tone: mixed
base: need:competence-.03
earth: the morning papers and the online notices after the opening
tribal: the elders speak of the telling the next morning, and the young ones repeat what they said
magic: the broadsheets are cried in the square the morning after the opening
times: per actor, director or writer with a show that opens: once a production; most professional openings and many amateur ones are reviewed somewhere (estimate)
window: 16-100
gap: 1-2
likelier: a professional opening in a city, a reviewer in the house on the first night, a lead part, a fringe show in festival week
rarer: a small amateur show in a village with no paper of its own, an opening in a week full of bigger news
scene: The morning after the opening, the notices are in: two papers, a website and a long post by someone who was in the third row. {N} sits at the kitchen table with a cup of coffee going cold, reading them one by one.
scene tribal: The morning after the telling, the elders speak of it by the cooking fires, and by noon the young ones are repeating what they said, the kind words and the sharp ones, all through the camp.
scene magic: The broadsheets are cried in the square the morning after the opening, and {N} buys all three from the boy at the corner and reads them on the playhouse steps.
- W | a duty done: the work was honest, whatever they wrote | impact: 1.0 | also: meaning+.05 | say: {N} reads the notices once, folds the paper away, and is at the theatre for the half as on any other night.
- U | a lesson: the critic saw what the director missed | impact: 0.9 | also: competence+.05 | say: {N} underlines the one sharp sentence, takes it into the next rehearsal, and changes the way the second act begins.
- B | a game: notices sell tickets, and nothing more | impact: 0.6 | also: autonomy+.05 | say: {N} checks the box office figures before the papers, and has the best line on the posters by Monday.
- R | a wound: one line that will be remembered for years | impact: 0.6 | also: belonging+.05 | say: {N} reads the cruel line over and over, then goes out with the company after the show, and by midnight they are laughing at it together.
- G | the way it goes: the local paper always says the same | impact: 0.9 | also: safety+.05 | say: {N} cuts the notice out for the family scrapbook, good or bad, and puts the kettle on.
